# Asides

## Close together
  {:label: sec-close}

The first paragraph has three asides within a few lines of each other, so that
in the margin column each one would run into the next :aside:a-one:: unless the
column pushes it down. The text goes on so that the second marker sits on the
next line :aside:a-two:: and the third one on the line after that, still close
enough to collide :aside:a-three::. A few more words to finish the paragraph and
give it a clear right edge to measure against.

:aside: {:label: a-one} The first aside. It is long enough to wrap onto several
lines in the margin column, so that it reaches down past the line of the second
marker and would overlap the second aside without the push-down. ::

:aside: {:label: a-two} The second aside, with some math: $x^2 + y^2 = 1$ holds on
the unit circle, and a few more words so it also wraps. ::

:aside: {:label: a-three} The third aside, short. ::

A paragraph between the first one and the figure, with no asides of its own,
long enough to wrap onto a second line at any reading width.

## Next to a caption
  {:label: sec-caption}

This paragraph calls an aside :aside:a-fig:: right before a figure, so the aside
and the figure's caption share the column and the caption is pushed down below
the aside when the two would overlap.

:aside: {:label: a-fig} An aside just before a figure. It wraps onto a few lines
in the margin column so that it reaches the top of the figure below and the
figure's caption has to move down to clear it. ::

:figure: {
  :label: fig-a
  :path: figure-light.png
  :scale: 0.3
}
:caption: The figure right below the paragraph with the aside.
::

## In a list and a proof
  {:label: sec-blocks}

:itemize:

  :-: A list item with an aside :aside:a-item:: in the middle of its text, which
  goes on a little longer.

  :-: A second item without one.

::

:theorem: {:label: thm-a} Every step of the proof below is easy.::

:proof: {:of: thm-a}
  :step: {:label: st-a} A first step with an aside :aside:a-step:: in its statement.::
  :step: A second step.::
::

## Two in one paragraph
  {:label: sec-two}

A paragraph with two asides, the first :aside:a-pair-1:: near the start and the
second :aside:a-pair-2:: a little later, so that on a narrow screen opening both
stacks them below the paragraph in marker order. The paragraph also has a footnote
:note:n-one:: that stays a footnote.

:aside: {:label: a-pair-2} The second aside of the pair. ::

:aside: {:label: a-pair-1} The first aside of the pair. ::

:note: {:label: n-one} A footnote, not an aside. ::

:aside: {:label: a-item} The aside of the list item. ::

:aside: {:label: a-step} The aside of the proof step. ::

The last paragraph of the document, so that every aside above has text after it.
It refers back to :ref:st-a:: so its preview can be checked.
